    "description": "# Future Directions: Spectral Shell Models of the Periodic Table\n\n## 1. Spin\u2013orbit splitting recovers the nuclear magic sequence\n\nConstruct a finite-dimensional shell Hamiltonian whose oscillator term has multiplicity `(N+1)(N+2)` and whose angular spin\u2013orbit perturbation is proportional to `L\u00b7S`. Conjecture that an explicit physically admissible ordering of the split levels has cumulative closures `2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126`.\n\nThe key insight is that the bare oscillator succeeds exactly through `20` and fails precisely where spin\u2013orbit splitting becomes decisive, so the discrepancy identifies the missing operator rather than merely showing numerical error.\n\nWhy now? The exact oscillator filling polynomial and its first divergence from the empirical sequence provide a sharply delimited baseline against which every proposed perturbation can be tested.\n\n## 2. Madelung ordering as a spectral perturbation theorem\n\nLet orbitals `(n,l)` have unperturbed Coulomb energy depending only on `n`, and perturb them by a term that is strictly increasing in `n+l`, with ties broken by `n`. Conjecture that there is a quantitative open region of perturbation parameters for which the first 118 orbital occupations follow the Madelung order.\n\nThe key insight is that the false Coulomb closures `2,10,28,60,110` arise from exact degeneracy in `l`; lifting that degeneracy in a controlled order can convert a hydrogenic spectrum into the observed block structure.\n\nWhy now? The cumulative Coulomb theorem isolates the degeneracy that must be broken and supplies exact gap sizes for perturbative inequalities.\n\n## 3. Stability of closed-shell counts under symmetry-preserving perturbations\n\nFor a finite self-adjoint Hamiltonian decomposed into irreducible angular-momentum sectors, conjecture that sufficiently small perturbations commuting with the symmetry action preserve cumulative shell multiplicities whenever adjacent spectral clusters have a positive gap.\n\nThe key insight is that periodic structure depends on spectral projections and their ranks, not on exact numerical eigenvalues; ranks should remain constant until a gap closes.\n\nWhy now? The diagonal shell model gives explicit eigenvectors and multiplicities, making it possible to formulate a quantitative gap condition and then broaden from diagonal matrices to general Hermitian operators.\n\n## 4. Woods\u2013Saxon spectral certification with interval bounds\n\nFor a radial Woods\u2013Saxon potential with a standard spin\u2013orbit term and parameter intervals calibrated to medium and heavy nuclei, conjecture that certified eigenvalue enclosures preserve the ordering needed for closures at `28`, `50`, and `82` throughout nontrivial parameter boxes.\n\nThe key insight is that a useful comparison with experiment requires robust eigenvalue inequalities over parameter ranges, not a single floating-point diagonalization.\n\nWhy now? The exact combinatorial shell counts identify the required multiplicities, while modern interval eigensolvers can test whether realistic potentials realize them stably.\n\n## 5. No universal energy-rounding scale recovers atomic number\n\nFor any fixed positive scale `E\u2080`, conjecture that nuclear binding energies across all stable isotopes cannot satisfy `round(E/E\u2080)=Z` consistently, even after choosing one representative isotope per element.\n\nThe key insight is that binding energy is controlled primarily by mass number and saturation, whereas atomic number is a charge count; a universal scalar rounding rule discards the independent neutron degree of freedom.\n\nWhy now? The shell-spectrum results clarify the valid role of multiplicity while separating it from the stronger and falsifiable claim that energy values themselves encode atomic numbers by uniform rounding.\n",
