    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## What was proved\n\n1. **Missingness is not itself a sheaf obstruction.** A partial database modeled as a partial function into a nonempty value type always has a total completion. If every cell is observed, the completion is unique; if a cell is missing and two distinct values exist, there are at least two completions.\n\n2. **Overlap constraints can be highly dependent.** On a triangle, one equality follows from the other two. More generally, all pairwise equality constraints on a nonempty family are equivalent to the constraints comparing every member with one fixed root.\n\n3. **Finite sheaf-style imputation is genuinely constrained optimization.** For finite cells and values, a minimizer of observed Hamming loss exists among assignments satisfying any finite family of equality constraints. Feasibility is guaranteed by constant assignments.\n\n4. **Distribution-free strict superiority is impossible.** On complete data, any observation-preserving mean-style and sheaf-style method both return the truth and therefore tie under any loss normalized to zero at the truth.\n\n## What was disproved\n\n1. **The universal probability formula `P(sheaf) = (1-r)^C`.** Under the natural partial-function model, completability has probability one for every missingness pattern, not `(1-r)^C`. At `r = 1/2`, `C = 1`, the proposed expression is already `1/2`.\n\n2. **Independent multiplication over all overlaps.** The Boolean triangle has three edge equations but only two independent ones. Its consistent fraction is `1/4`, not the `1/8` predicted by multiplying three independent half-probabilities.\n\n3. **A distribution-free claim that sheaf imputation is always strictly better for `r < 0.5` and more than ten features.** Missing rate zero lies in this range, and complete data forces a tie between methods that preserve observations. Any valid performance theorem must specify a stochastic model and should use non-strict or expected-risk comparisons.\n\n## Next formal developments\n\n* Replace equality constraints by restriction maps of a finite cellular sheaf. Characterize feasibility as membership in the kernel of a coboundary map and derive exact counts over finite fields from its rank.\n* Prove the graph formula for a constant `q`-valued sheaf: the number of consistent assignments is `q` raised to the number of connected components. This would quantify precisely how cycles create redundant edge constraints.\n* Introduce a probability space for missingness and noisy observations. Derive probabilities from the specified observation model rather than solely from `r` and a raw constraint count.\n* Formalize squared-error imputation over finite-dimensional real vector spaces, with existence from closedness/coercivity or orthogonal projection onto the global-section subspace.\n* State method-comparison results in terms of expected risk under an explicit generative model. Without assumptions connecting the sheaf restrictions to ground truth, no uniform ranking over mean, KNN, MICE, and constrained methods can hold.\n* Study inconsistent local observations through least-squares residuals and cohomological obstruction certificates, separating ordinary missing data from genuinely conflicting overlap data.\n",
