    "description": "# Future Directions for Exotic Positional Arithmetic\n\n## 1. Canonical binary expansions in imaginary quadratic rings\n\n**Conjecture.** Classify all quadratic algebraic integers `\u03b2` of norm two for which the digit set `{0,1}` gives unique finite expansions of every element of the ambient imaginary quadratic integer ring. The classification should be expressible through the geometry of a finite exceptional region for norm descent.\n\nThe key insight is that the proved base `i \u2212 1` theorem reduces the infinite lattice to parity extraction, strict norm decrease, and five exceptional points; the same three-part mechanism should distinguish the successful quadratic radices.\n\n**Why now?** Complete negabinary and Gaussian base theorems provide one-dimensional and two-dimensional models, making a uniform classification both concrete and falsifiable.\n\n## 2. A transducer between Zeckendorf and phinary normal forms\n\n**Conjecture.** There is a terminating, confluent finite-state rewrite system that converts every Zeckendorf representation into a finite base-`\u03c6` expansion with digits `0` and `1`, no adjacent ones, and bounded negative support; its output is canonical among expansions satisfying a fixed endpoint convention.\n\nThe key insight is that both systems normalize by the same local relation, `011 \u2194 100`, but Zeckendorf indices and phinary exponents impose different boundary conditions.\n\n**Why now?** Uniqueness of nonconsecutive Fibonacci sums and the golden-ratio carry law are established independently, making confluence and endpoint control the remaining testable bridge.\n\n## 3. Negative-base uniqueness over Euclidean domains\n\n**Conjecture.** Let `R` be a Euclidean domain and let `\u03b2 \u2208 R` have finite residue ring. If a complete residue digit set contains zero and admits a strictly decreasing Euclidean digit-extraction map outside a finite exceptional set, then every element of `R` has a canonical finite base-`\u03b2` expansion; uniqueness is equivalent to uniqueness of residues at every extraction step.\n\nThe key insight is that existence and uniqueness separate cleanly: descent proves termination, while residue injectivity peels equal expansions one digit at a time.\n\n**Why now?** Negabinary arithmetic supplies the complete one-dimensional model, while complex bases provide immediate nontrivial test cases over `\u2124[i]`.\n\n## 4. Carry complexity as a radix-selection invariant\n\n**Conjecture.** Among canonical radix systems of fixed residue alphabet size, the expected number of local rewrites required to normalize the sum of two length-`n` random numerals has a linear asymptotic whose coefficient is determined by the stationary distribution of a finite carry automaton.\n\nThe key insight is that an alien civilization's preferred base may be governed less by numeral length than by normalization cost, which can be modeled as an ergodic statistic of carry states.\n\n**Why now?** Mixed-radix Horner evaluation, negabinary parity extraction, and phinary rewriting offer three sharply different systems on which the proposed invariant can be calculated and compared.\n\n## 5. Prime-pattern invariance under computable radix changes\n\n**Conjecture.** No computable positional radix system with bounded digits, unique finite normal forms, and finite-state addition can make primality eventually recognizable by a regular language of digit strings.\n\nThe key insight is that a genuinely \u201csimple\u201d prime pattern should be defined by automata-theoretic complexity rather than visual appearance; closure properties of automatic sets should then conflict with the multiplicative irregularity of the primes.\n\n**Why now?** The explicit canonical systems developed here make the admissibility, uniqueness, and finite-state hypotheses precise enough to separate meaningful prime-pattern claims from coordinate artifacts such as spiral plots.\n",
